\chapter{Context-Free Grammars and Earley Parsing}
\label{ch:cfg-earley}

DSLtk ships two fundamentally different parsing technologies, and they sit at
opposite ends of the design space. The PEG subsystem of
\chapref{ch:peg-definition} is a \emph{deterministic} formalism: rules are
ordered, an ordered choice commits to its first alternative that can match,
and the matcher never explores more than one path through the grammar at a
time. That determinism is what makes PEG parsing fast and what makes its
error messages good, and it is also its main limitation. A PEG cannot be
left-recursive, because the matcher would recurse forever before it could
ever consume a token; it cannot express the unambiguous context-free
grammar of a natural language, because ordered choice has no notion of
ambiguity to resolve; and it cannot express a grammar that is genuinely
ambiguous, because the order of the alternatives silently becomes part of the
language definition.

The facility in this chapter takes the opposite approach. A grammar is an
ordinary \emph{context-free grammar}: a set of nonterminals, each of which
may expand in several ways, and a set of terminals. Such a grammar may be
left-recursive, may contain epsilon productions, and may be ambiguous, and
all three are perfectly legal. The parser is the Earley recogniser, which
can handle any context-free grammar in \(O(n^{3})\) time, and which builds
the parse tree as it goes.

The price of that generality is speed and precision. Earley parsing is
markedly slower than PEG matching, and the ambiguity resolution policy is
crude: when a grammar admits more than one derivation, the parser keeps the
first one it discovers and discards the rest. That policy is deterministic,
which is a genuine virtue --- the same input always yields the same tree ---
but it means the rule order in the grammar is part of the semantics, and it
means the tree you get back is not necessarily the one a language designer
had in mind. Section~\ref{sec:ch33-ambiguity} returns to this.

One more design decision governs everything else, and it is stated in the
header comment above \api{dsl::CFG}: \emph{``Grammar terminals are tokens,
not characters. The user's lexer chooses the token syntax.''} A terminal in
this system is matched by \emph{string equality} against the text of the
next token in the input. The grammar therefore never sees the raw characters
of the source; it sees whatever the user's lexer chose to produce. This is
the right design for a language toolkit, because every real language has a
lexer anyway, and it means the same grammar can be fed by a hand-written
lexer, by one built from \api{dsl::pattern<>}, or by a lexer that emits
symbolic tokens for a compiler front end.

\section{A worked example}

The program below defines one small grammar twice --- once with typed
productions and once as an S-expression --- parses a token list with both,
and shows that they agree. It then exercises the parts of the facility that
are easy to get wrong: a failed parse with its diagnostic, an ambiguous
grammar, a left-recursive grammar and an epsilon production.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={Defining a grammar two ways and parsing tokens into an \code{ASTNode}.},label={lst:ch33-grammar}]
#include "DSLtk.hpp"

#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

namespace
{

// A tiny lexer for the example language.  Grammar terminals are token
// texts, so the lexer decides what a token looks like.
std::vector<std::string>
lex (const std::string &source)
{
  std::vector<std::string> tokens;
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < source.size ();)
    {
      if (source[i] == ' ')
        {
          ++i;
          continue;
        }
      if (source[i] == '(' || source[i] == ')')
        {
          tokens.push_back (std::string (1, source[i]));
          ++i;
          continue;
        }
      auto begin = i;
      while (i < source.size () && source[i] != ' ' && source[i] != '('
             && source[i] != ')')
        ++i;
      tokens.push_back (source.substr (begin, i - begin));
    }
  return tokens;
}

} // namespace

int
main ()
{
  // ---- the grammar, written with typed productions --------------------
  using Formula = dsl::CFGNonterminal<"Formula">;
  using Atom = dsl::CFGNonterminal<"Atom">;
  using True = dsl::CFGTerminal<"true">;
  using False = dsl::CFGTerminal<"false">;
  using Not = dsl::CFGTerminal<"not">;
  using Open = dsl::CFGTerminal<"(">;
  using Close = dsl::CFGTerminal<")">;

  auto typed = dsl::make_cfg<"Formula"> (
      dsl::CFGProduction<"Formula", Atom> (),
      dsl::CFGProduction<"Atom", True> (),
      dsl::CFGProduction<"Atom", False> (),
      dsl::CFGProduction<"Atom", Not, Atom> (),
      dsl::CFGProduction<"Atom", Open, Formula, Close> ());

  // ---- the same grammar, written as an S-expression --------------------
  auto sexpr = dsl::sexpr_grammar (R"sexpr(
    (grammar
      (start Formula)
      (rule Formula (Atom))
      (rule Atom ("true") ("false") ("not" Atom) ("(" Formula ")")))
  )sexpr");

  auto tokens = lex ("not not true");
  std::cout << "tokens:";
  for (auto &token : tokens)
    std::cout << ' ' << token;
  std::cout << '\n';

  auto result = typed.parse (tokens);
  std::cout << "typed ok: " << result.ok () << '\n';
  if (result.tree)
    std::cout << "typed tree: " << result.tree->dump () << '\n';

  auto other = sexpr.parse (tokens);
  std::cout << "sexpr ok: " << other.ok () << '\n';
  if (other.tree)
    std::cout << "sexpr tree: " << other.tree->dump () << '\n';

  std::cout << "the two trees agree: "
            << (result.tree && other.tree && *result.tree == *other.tree
                    ? "yes"
                    : "no")
            << '\n';

  // ---- a failure ------------------------------------------------------
  auto bad = typed.parse (lex ("not"));
  std::cout << "bad ok: " << bad.ok () << '\n';
  std::cout << "bad position: " << bad.position << '\n';
  std::cout << "bad expected:";
  for (auto &name : bad.expected)
    std::cout << ' ' << name;
  std::cout << '\n';

  // ---- an ambiguous grammar: the first derivation discovered wins ------
  dsl::CFG ambiguous;
  ambiguous.start = "S";
  ambiguous.add ("S", { dsl::CFGSymbol::nonterminal ("A") });
  ambiguous.add ("S", { dsl::CFGSymbol::nonterminal ("B") });
  ambiguous.add ("A", { dsl::CFGSymbol::nonterminal ("C") });
  ambiguous.add ("B", { dsl::CFGSymbol::nonterminal ("C") });
  ambiguous.add ("C", { dsl::CFGSymbol::token ("x") });

  auto amb = ambiguous.parse (std::vector<std::string> { "x" });
  std::cout << "ambiguous tree: "
            << (amb.tree ? amb.tree->dump () : "<none>") << '\n';

  // ---- a left-recursive grammar ---------------------------------------
  dsl::CFG left;
  left.start = "E";
  left.add ("E", { dsl::CFGSymbol::nonterminal ("E"),
                   dsl::CFGSymbol::token ("+"),
                   dsl::CFGSymbol::nonterminal ("T") });
  left.add ("E", { dsl::CFGSymbol::nonterminal ("T") });
  left.add ("T", { dsl::CFGSymbol::token ("n") });

  auto lr = left.parse (std::vector<std::string> { "n", "+", "n" });
  std::cout << "left recursive tree: "
            << (lr.tree ? lr.tree->dump () : "<none>") << '\n';

  // ---- an epsilon production ------------------------------------------
  dsl::CFG nullable;
  nullable.start = "S";
  nullable.add ("S", { dsl::CFGSymbol::nonterminal ("A"),
                       dsl::CFGSymbol::token ("x") });
  nullable.add ("A", {});

  auto eps = nullable.parse (std::vector<std::string> { "x" });
  std::cout << "epsilon tree: "
            << (eps.tree ? eps.tree->dump () : "<none>") << '\n';

  return 0;
}
\end{lstlisting}

The output is:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcmd,caption={Output of Listing~\ref{lst:ch33-grammar}.},label={lst:ch33-output}]
tokens: not not true
typed ok: 1
typed tree: (Formula (Atom (token not) (Atom (token not) (Atom (token true)))))
sexpr ok: 1
sexpr tree: (Formula (Atom (token not) (Atom (token not) (Atom (token true)))))
the two trees agree: yes
bad ok: 0
bad position: 1
bad expected: true false not (
ambiguous tree: (S (A (C (token x))))
left recursive tree: (E (E (T (token n))) (token +) (T (token n)))
epsilon tree: (S (A) (token x))
\end{lstlisting}

Three details in that listing are worth flagging before the machinery is
described.

The first is a syntactic wart. \code{make_cfg} takes its productions
\emph{by value}, so each production must be spelled with a trailing pair of
parentheses: \code{dsl::CFGProduction<"Atom", True> ()}. Writing
\code{dsl::CFGProduction<"Atom", True>} without the parentheses does not
compile, because a template-id is a type and not an expression. The
production types are empty structs, so the parentheses simply value-initialise
a temporary, and the factory then calls the static \code{rule()} member on
it.

The second is that the two spellings of the grammar really are the same
grammar. The typed version and the S-expression version produce byte-identical
trees, which is the check that matters: the S-expression front end is a
convenience, not a second implementation.

The third is the ambiguity line. The grammar \code{S -> A | B},
\code{A -> C}, \code{B -> C}, \code{C -> "x"} has two derivations
of the single token \code{x}, and the parser returns the one that goes
through \code{A}. Section~\ref{sec:ch33-ambiguity} explains why, and what
that means in practice.

\section{The data model}

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{@{}L{0.26\textwidth}L{0.66\textwidth}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Type} & \textbf{Members and meaning} \\
\midrule
\code{CFGSymbol} &
A grammar symbol: a \code{bool terminal} and a \code{std::string name}.
Built by \code{CFGSymbol::token (text)}, which sets \code{terminal} to
\code{true}, and \code{CFGSymbol::nonterminal (name)}, which sets it to
\code{false}. \\
\code{CFGRule} &
One production: a left-hand side \code{std::string lhs} and a
\code{std::vector<CFGSymbol> rhs}. One rule is exactly one alternative;
several alternatives for the same nonterminal are several rules. \\
\code{CFGParseResult} &
The outcome of a parse: an \code{std::optional<ASTNode> tree}, a
\code{std::size_t position}, and a \code{std::vector<std::string> expected}
of terminal names. The \code{ok()} member reports whether the tree is
present. \\
\code{CFG} &
The grammar itself: a public \code{std::string start} and a public
\code{std::vector<CFGRule> productions}, plus \code{add (lhs, rhs)},
\code{validate ()} and \code{parse (tokens)}. \\
\code{CFGTerminal<Text>} &
A compile-time terminal. Exposes \code{static CFGSymbol symbol ()}. \\
\code{CFGNonterminal<Name>} &
A compile-time nonterminal. Exposes \code{static CFGSymbol symbol ()}. \\
\code{CFGProduction<LHS, Symbols...>} &
A compile-time production. Exposes \code{static CFGRule rule ()}. \\
\code{make_cfg<Start>(...)} &
Free function template that assembles a \code{CFG} from production types and
validates it. \\
\code{sexpr_grammar(...)} &
Free function that builds a \code{CFG} from an S-expression, either as a
\code{dsl::SExpr} or as a \code{std::string_view}. \\
\midrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{The context-free grammar API.}
\label{tab:ch33-api}
\end{table}

Note what is \emph{not} in that table. There is no \code{recognize ()}
member; recognition is \code{parse (tokens).ok ()}. There is no
\code{describe ()}, no \code{first_set ()}, no \code{follow_set ()}, and no
way to ask the grammar which nonterminals it mentions. The grammar is a
plain data structure, and the parser is the only thing that interrogates it.

\code{CFGParseResult} is the whole diagnostic surface. On success the tree
is present and \code{position} equals the number of tokens consumed, which
is always all of them. On failure the tree is absent, \code{position} is the
furthest position at which the parser made progress, and \code{expected}
lists the terminals that the items sitting at that position were waiting
for. The special name \code{"<end-of-input>"} is appended when the parser
reached the end of the input but the grammar wanted more.

\section{Three ways to write a grammar}

\subsection{Typed productions}

The typed form is the most direct. Each symbol is a type alias for one of
two templates, and each rule is a type alias for \code{CFGProduction}.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={A grammar written with typed productions. The fragment assumes that \code{DSLtk.hpp} has been included.},label={lst:ch33-typed}]
using Formula = dsl::CFGNonterminal<"Formula">;
using Atom    = dsl::CFGNonterminal<"Atom">;
using True    = dsl::CFGTerminal<"true">;
using Not     = dsl::CFGTerminal<"not">;

auto grammar = dsl::make_cfg<"Formula"> (
    dsl::CFGProduction<"Formula", Atom> (),
    dsl::CFGProduction<"Atom", True> (),
    dsl::CFGProduction<"Atom", Not, Atom> ());
\end{lstlisting}

The advantage of this form is that the symbol names are checked by the
compiler: a misspelled nonterminal is a different type, and a terminal used
where a nonterminal was intended is a different type. The disadvantage is
that it is verbose, and that the grammar is spread across a dozen type
aliases.

\subsection{An S-expression grammar}

The S-expression form is more compact and is the natural choice for a
grammar that is stored as data rather than written in code. The syntax is
\code{(grammar (start Expr) (rule Expr (Expr "+" Term) (Term)) ...)}.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslsexpr,caption={A grammar written as an S-expression.},label={lst:ch33-sexpr}]
(grammar
  (start Formula)
  (rule Formula (Atom))
  (rule Atom ("true") ("false") ("not" Atom) ("(" Formula ")")))
\end{lstlisting}

The convention that makes this work is short but essential:
\emph{quoted atoms are terminals, unquoted atoms are nonterminals}. A
quoted atom such as \code{"not"} becomes a terminal whose text is
\code{not}; an unquoted atom such as \code{Atom} becomes a nonterminal.
This is exactly the convention the S-expression reader already uses
elsewhere in DSLtk, and it means the same reader, the same quoting rules
and the same \code{;} comment syntax apply to grammars as to rewrite rules.

Two consequences follow. First, an operator terminal \emph{must} be quoted:
an unquoted \code{+} would be read as a nonterminal named \code{+}, and the
grammar would fail validation with \code{"undefined CFG nonterminal +"}.
Second, an alternative is always a list, even when it contains a single
symbol, so a rule whose right-hand side is one terminal is written
\code{("true")} and not \code{"true"}; writing the latter raises
\code{"CFG alternative must be a list"}.

An empty list \code{()} is the epsilon production. The
\code{(rule A ())} alternative produces a rule with an empty right-hand
side, which is how nullable nonterminals are written.

Each alternative of a \code{(rule ...)} becomes a separate \code{CFGRule},
so \code{(rule Atom ("true") ("false"))} contributes two rules, both with
left-hand side \code{Atom}.

\subsection{Programmatically}

The third route is to fill in the public members directly. This is the
route to take when the grammar is computed rather than written.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={A grammar assembled programmatically. The fragment is a statement sequence and belongs inside a function body.},label={lst:ch33-programmatic}]
dsl::CFG grammar;
grammar.start = "S";
grammar.add ("S", { dsl::CFGSymbol::nonterminal ("A") });
grammar.add ("S", { dsl::CFGSymbol::nonterminal ("B") });
grammar.add ("A", { dsl::CFGSymbol::nonterminal ("C") });
grammar.add ("B", { dsl::CFGSymbol::nonterminal ("C") });
grammar.add ("C", { dsl::CFGSymbol::token ("x") });
\end{lstlisting}

\code{add} takes its arguments by value and appends to \code{productions}.
Note that the programmatic route does not validate: \code{validate ()} is
called by \code{make_cfg} and by \code{sexpr_grammar}, but a grammar built
by hand is only checked when \code{parse} is called, because \code{parse}
begins with a call to \code{validate ()}.

\subsection{What validation checks}

\code{CFG::validate ()} performs three checks and throws
\code{std::invalid_argument} if any of them fails.

\begin{itemize}
\item Every left-hand side must be non-empty; otherwise the error is
\code{"empty CFG nonterminal name"}.
\item The start symbol must be defined by some rule; otherwise the error is
\code{"undefined CFG start symbol"}.
\item Every nonterminal appearing on a right-hand side must be defined by
some rule; otherwise the error is
\code{"undefined CFG nonterminal "} followed by the name.
\end{itemize}

Terminals are not checked, because a terminal is matched by string equality
against the input and there is nothing to define. A grammar may mention a
terminal that never appears in any input; it simply never matches.

\section{The Earley algorithm}

The parser is an implementation of the classical Earley recogniser with
tree construction grafted on. It maintains a \emph{chart}: one set of items
per input position. Position zero is before the first token, position
\(k\) is after the first \(k\) tokens, so there are \(n+1\) sets for \(n\)
tokens.

Each item records four things.

\begin{description}
\item[\code{rule}] the index of the production the item is an instance of.
\item[\code{dot}] the position in that production's right-hand side, i.e.\ how
much of the production has been recognised so far.
\item[\code{origin}] the input position at which the left-hand side of the
production began.
\item[\code{children}] the sub-trees collected so far, one per symbol
already passed by the dot.
\end{description}

A production \code{A -> B C} at dot position one with origin three is
written \code{A -> B . C}, meaning that the parser is in the middle of
matching \code{A}, has already matched a \code{B} beginning at position
three, and is now looking for a \code{C}.

Before parsing begins, the grammar is copied and an augmented rule
\code{$start -> start} is inserted at index zero. The parser then starts
from the item \code{$start -> . start} at position zero with origin zero.
On success the tree that is returned is the child of that augmented item ---
that is, the tree is rooted at the start nonterminal, and the artificial
\code{$start} node never appears in the output.

