Figure~\ref{fig:ch33-chart} shows the chart for a tiny grammar. The three
columns are the three input positions; the dotted arrows mark the tokens
consumed on the way from one column to the next. Notice that the chart is
not a simple path: the column at position one contains three items, because
the parser has not yet decided whether the \code{S} it is building will be
completed with a \code{B} or not.

\subsection{The three operations}

The heart of the algorithm is a loop over positions, and within a position a
loop over the items of that position's set. Each item is handled by exactly
one of three operations, determined by what the dot is pointing at.

\textbf{Scanning} applies when the dot points at a terminal. If the
terminal's name is equal to the text of the token at the current position,
the dot advances by one, a child \code{ASTNode ("token", token-text)} is
appended, and the advanced item is inserted into the set for the next
position. If the names differ, nothing happens: the item simply dies.

\textbf{Prediction} applies when the dot points at a nonterminal. Every rule
whose left-hand side is that nonterminal is inserted into the current
position's set with the dot at zero and the origin at the current position.
This is the step that makes left recursion work: the rule \code{E -> E +
T} predicts itself, producing an item \code{E -> . E + T} at the same
position, and the insertion guard described below refuses to insert it a
second time, so the recursion terminates.

Prediction is followed by an \emph{epsilon fix-up}. If a nullable production
of the predicted nonterminal has already been completed at this position,
then the item being predicted can advance past the nonterminal immediately.
The fix-up searches the current position's set for items that are complete,
whose origin is the current position and whose left-hand side is the
predicted nonterminal, and for each of them inserts an advanced copy of the
item under consideration with the completed sub-tree as the new child.

\textbf{Completion} applies when the dot is at the end of the right-hand
side. The item's collected children are wrapped in a node
\code{ASTNode (rule.lhs, children)}, and then every item in the set at the
item's \emph{origin} that was waiting for this nonterminal is advanced: its
dot moves past the nonterminal and the new sub-tree becomes its child. This
is the step that propagates a completed nonterminal back to its parents.

\begin{pseudocode}[Predict, scan and complete]
\textbf{function} process (chart, pos, item)\\
\quad rule = rules[item.rule]\\
\quad \textbf{if} item.dot equals the length of rule.rhs\\
\qquad \qquad \textbf{// completion}\\
\qquad \qquad tree = ASTNode(rule.lhs, item.children)\\
\qquad \qquad \textbf{for} each waiting in chart[item.origin]\\
\qquad \qquad \qquad \textbf{if} waiting expects the nonterminal rule.lhs\\
\qquad \qquad \qquad\qquad waiting.dot = waiting.dot plus 1\\
\qquad \qquad \qquad\qquad append tree to waiting.children\\
\qquad \qquad \qquad\qquad insert (chart, pos, waiting)\\
\quad \textbf{else if} rule.rhs[item.dot] is a terminal\\
\qquad \qquad \textbf{// scanning}\\
\qquad \qquad \textbf{if} pos is less than the token count\\
\qquad \qquad \qquad \textbf{and} rule.rhs[item.dot].name equals tokens[pos]\\
\qquad \qquad \qquad\qquad item.dot = item.dot plus 1\\
\qquad \qquad \qquad\qquad append ASTNode("token", tokens[pos])\\
\qquad \qquad \qquad\qquad insert (chart, pos plus 1, item)\\
\quad \textbf{else}\\
\qquad \qquad \textbf{// prediction}\\
\qquad \qquad \textbf{for} each rule with the same left-hand side\\
\qquad \qquad \qquad\qquad insert (chart, pos, item with dot zero)\\
\qquad \qquad \textbf{// epsilon fix-up}\\
\qquad \qquad \textbf{for} each completed in chart[pos]\\
\qquad \qquad \qquad\qquad \textbf{if} completed derives the predicted nonterminal\\
\qquad \qquad \qquad\qquad\qquad \textbf{then} advance the item with it
\end{pseudocode}

\subsection{The insertion guard}

The single most important detail in the implementation is the insertion
guard. Every insertion into the chart goes through a lambda that first
scans the target set for an item with the same triple of
\code{(rule, dot, origin)}; if one exists, the new item is discarded.

\begin{pseudocode}[The chart insertion guard]
\textbf{function} insert (chart, position, item)\\
\quad \textbf{for} each old in chart[position]\\
\qquad \qquad \textbf{if} old.rule equals item.rule\\
\qquad \qquad \qquad \textbf{and} old.dot equals item.dot\\
\qquad \qquad \qquad \textbf{and} old.origin equals item.origin\\
\qquad \qquad \qquad\qquad \textbf{return}\\
\quad append item to chart[position]
\end{pseudocode}

This guard has two distinct jobs, and it is worth keeping them apart,
because only one of them is about ambiguity.

The first job is termination. Without the guard, prediction of a
left-recursive rule would insert the same item forever. The guard turns
the infinite tree of possible derivations into a finite chart. This is
precisely what distinguishes Earley parsing from the naive recursive
descent that a left-recursive grammar would otherwise provoke.

The second job is ambiguity resolution. Two different derivations of the
same nonterminal at the same position would, in general, produce items with
the same \code{(rule, dot, origin)} triple but different children. The
guard keeps the first and discards the second. Since the guard is applied
at every insertion --- not just at prediction --- the first derivation
discovered for a given configuration wins throughout, and the tree that
emerges is the first derivation the search happened to reach.

\subsection{The main loop}

The outer loop runs over the positions, and the inner loop runs over the
items of the current position's set. The inner loop's bound is
\code{chart[pos].size ()}, re-evaluated on every iteration, and the item is
\emph{copied} out of the set before it is processed.

That re-evaluation is essential rather than an optimisation. Prediction
inserts into the current position's set, and so does the epsilon fix-up;
those items must themselves be processed before the parser moves on to the
next position, or nullable productions and left recursion would be handled
incorrectly. Because the bound is re-read, the loop naturally extends to
cover them. Copying the item before processing it is what allows the
processing to append to the very set being iterated over without
invalidating the iteration.

\subsection{Reading the result}

When the loop finishes, the parser looks at the last set in the chart --- the
one at position \(n\) --- and searches it for the item with rule index zero
(the augmented rule), dot one and origin zero. If that item is there, the
parse succeeded: the tree is that item's first child, and the position is
the token count.

If the item is not there, the parse failed. The parser then finds the
largest position whose set is not empty and reports that as the failure
position, and collects the names of the terminals that the items in that set
were waiting for, de-duplicated. If no item was waiting for a terminal but
the parser stopped before the end of the input, the list
\code{"<end-of-input>"} is appended, because the only way forward from that
point would have been to stop.

\section{Why Earley, and what it costs}

\subsection{Left recursion, epsilon and ambiguity}

The three properties that distinguish Earley parsing from a recursive-descent
parser are all consequences of the chart.

\textbf{Left recursion} is handled because the parser never recurses. The
rule \code{E -> E + T} is not expanded by calling the parser on \code{E};
it is expanded by predicting an item \code{E -> . E + T} in the current
chart set, and the guard then prevents that item from being predicted
again. The result is a chart that represents the left-recursive derivation
without any call stack, and the tree that comes out is left-associative:
parsing \code{n + n} with \code{E -> E + T | T} and
\code{T -> n} yields \code{(E (E (T (token n))) (token +) (T (token n)))},
as the listing shows. A recursive-descent parser written in the obvious way
would loop forever on that grammar; a parser written with the usual
left-factoring trick would need the grammar to be rewritten by hand.

\textbf{Epsilon productions} are handled by two mechanisms working together.
The prediction step inserts an item for a nullable rule at the current
position, and because that item's dot is immediately at the end of its
right-hand side, it is completed there and then. The epsilon fix-up in the
prediction step handles the case where the completion of a nullable
production has already happened before the item that needs it was processed,
by advancing that item past the nonterminal directly. The listing shows the
grammar \code{S -> A x} with \code{A} nullable parsing the single token
\code{x} into \code{(S (A) (token x))}: the \code{A} node appears with no
children, because it derived the empty string.

\textbf{Ambiguity} is not solved but rather decided. See
Section~\ref{sec:ch33-ambiguity}.

\subsection{The cost}

The classical bound for Earley parsing is \(O(n^{3})\) time for an input of
\(n\) tokens and a grammar of bounded size. The cubic term comes from the
completion step: every completed item scans the entire set at its origin in
order to find the items waiting for it, and there are \(O(n^{2})\) items in
the chart overall.

The chart itself holds \(O(n^{2})\) items, since an item is identified by a
rule, a dot and an origin, and there are \(O(n)\) positions and \(O(n)\)
origins. Each item additionally carries a \code{std::vector<ASTNode>} of
children, and each child is a whole sub-tree, so the memory used by the
parser is dominated by the tree being built rather than by the chart
structure. On a grammar with many long productions this is a substantial
allocation load.

For comparison, a PEG matcher consumes each token once per rule and never
allocates a chart, so it is typically an order of magnitude faster on the
same input. The Earley facility earns its cost only when the grammar
genuinely needs the generality: left recursion, epsilon, or ambiguity that a
PEG cannot express at all.

\section{Ambiguity: the first derivation wins}
\label{sec:ch33-ambiguity}

Consider the grammar of the listing: \code{S -> A | B},
\code{A -> C}, \code{B -> C}, \code{C -> x}. The token \code{x} has
two derivations, through \code{A} and through \code{B}, and the parser
returns \code{(S (A (C (token x))))}.

The reason is the guard. Both derivations eventually try to insert the item
\code{S -> A .} or \code{S -> B .} at the same position with the same
origin, and more importantly both try to advance the augmented item
\code{-> . S} at position zero with a completed \code{S}. The
first advance wins the race; the second is refused because the triple
\code{(rule 0, dot 1, origin 0)} is already present at that position. The
derivation that reached the guard first is the one that is kept.

Three consequences follow, and they are worth stating precisely.

First, the policy is \emph{deterministic}. The same grammar and the same
input always produce the same tree, on every run and on every platform.
That is a real property, and it is what distinguishes this from a parser
that returns an arbitrary member of the set of parse trees.

Second, the policy is \emph{order-dependent}. The grammar's rule order is
part of the semantics. In the example, the rule \code{S -> A} was added
before \code{S -> B}, and the derivation through \code{A} was found
first. Swapping the two rules would swap the result. An ambiguous grammar
is therefore a grammar whose meaning depends on how it was written down,
which is exactly the property that makes ambiguity dangerous in practice.

Third, the policy is \emph{not} a disambiguation by precedence or
associativity. There is no notion of a preferred alternative beyond "the one
the search reached first", and no way to ask for a different one. If a
grammar is ambiguous and the tree you want is not the one you get, the
answer is to remove the ambiguity from the grammar --- by factoring the
common prefix, by introducing a precedence nonterminal, or by splitting the
terminal vocabulary so that the two derivations are distinguishable.

\section{Edge cases and limits}

\begin{warnbox}[Terminals are token texts]
A terminal in this system matches a token by \emph{string equality}. There
is no way to say ``a terminal that matches any token whose text looks like
an integer''. If the lexer emits the text \code{number} for every numeric
literal, then the grammar can have a terminal \code{"number"}, but the tree
will record the token text \code{number} and not the literal value. A
grammar that needs to see the value of a numeric literal must therefore
either put the value into the token text and match it exactly, or use a
different mechanism --- such as a PEG or a hand-written recursive-descent
parser --- to carry the value alongside the tree.
\end{warnbox}

The remaining limits are as follows.

\textbf{No error recovery.} A failed parse yields a position and a list of
expected terminals, and nothing else. There is no attempt to resynchronise,
no partial tree, and no list of every failure. For a compiler front end
this is usually what you want; for an interactive editor it is not.

\textbf{The expected list is derived, not predicted.} The names in
\code{expected} are the terminals that the items sitting at the failure
position happened to be waiting for. They are a good approximation of what
the grammar could have accepted there, but they are not a complete
first-set computation, and they will not mention a terminal that the
grammar could have accepted through a derivation that was pruned by the
guard.

\textbf{Empty input.} Parsing an empty token list succeeds if and only if
the start symbol is nullable. The tree in that case is the start symbol
with no children. The empty string is not a special case anywhere in the
implementation.

\textbf{Grammar size is not checked against the input.} The parser makes no
attempt to detect useless or unreachable rules, and it does not report
rules that can never match. A grammar with a typo in a terminal name simply
never matches that terminal.

\textbf{Validation is shallow.} \code{validate ()} checks that the start
symbol is defined and that every nonterminal on a right-hand side is
defined. It does not check for left recursion, for ambiguity, for
unreachable symbols, or for the possibility of an infinite derivation. A
grammar that is valid under \code{validate ()} may still be useless, and
the parser will simply fail on every input.

\textbf{No left factoring is performed.} If two rules for the same
nonterminal share a prefix, the parser will explore both, which is correct
but quadratic in the shared prefix length. Writing the grammar in factored
form is the user's job.

\textbf{The tree is not shared.} Every completed item allocates a fresh
\code{ASTNode} for its sub-tree, so a grammar in which the same
nonterminal is derived many times produces many copies of the same tree. For
large inputs this is the dominant cost, and it is the reason the graph-based
facilities of \chapref{ch:terms-and-reduction} exist: they encode a term
once and share the repeated parts.

\section{Closing}

The context-free grammar facility is DSLtk's answer to the question that
the PEG subsystem cannot answer: how do you write the grammar you actually
mean? It accepts left-recursive rules, nullable rules and ambiguous rules,
it builds an \code{ASTNode} as it recognises, and it produces the same tree
every time for the same input. What it gives up in exchange is speed, and
the ability to choose between competing derivations.

The three ways of writing a grammar --- typed productions, an
S-expression, and programmatic assembly --- are three spellings of the same
data structure, and the first two exist because grammars are often better
written down than computed. The parser itself is a textbook Earley
recogniser with tree construction attached, and the insertion guard that
makes it terminate is also the thing that decides what happens when a
grammar admits more than one derivation.

With this chapter the book has covered DSLtk's parsing facilities. The next
chapter moves up one more level of abstraction, from languages that are
parsed to languages that are \emph{written}: a built-in preprocessor with
macros, conditionals, includes and an optional scripting hook.
